\f1\fs22 Sublime Point is a scenic lookout located in the Blue Mountains of New South Wales, Australia, offering panoramic views of the Jamison Valley. The lookout is situated on a sandstone plateau, which is part of the larger Sydney Basin geological province. The sandstone at Sublime Point was formed from ancient sand dunes that were compressed and cemented over millions of years to form the solid rock layer visible today. From the lookout, visitors can see the Jamison Valley stretched out below, characterized by deep gorges, rolling hills, and lush vegetation. The valley is also home to the famous Three Sisters rock formation, which is visible from Sublime Point. The breathtaking views and geological history make Sublime Point a popular tourist destination.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Jamison Valley was carved out of the plateau over millions of years through the erosive forces of weather and water. The valley was formed as a result of the upward and downward movements of the Earth's crust, causing the sandstone plateau to crack and erode over time. The fast-flowing streams and rivers in the area further eroded the soft rock layers, deepening the valleys and gorges. The process of erosion continues to shape the landscape to this day, making the Jamison Valley a dynamic and ever-changing environment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The high point surrounding the Jamison Valley in the Blue Mountains of New South Wales, Australia, is primarily composed of sandstone, which is part of the larger Sydney Basin geological province. The sandstone was formed from ancient sand dunes that were compressed and cemented over millions of years, creating the solid rock layer visible today. The sandstone is characterized by its distinctive layering, varying colors, and rich textures. In addition to sandstone, other rock types, such as shale and mudstone, can also be found in the area. These rocks were formed from sediment that accumulated in ancient lakes and oceans and were later compressed and cemented over time. The presence of these different rock types provides a unique geological record of the area's history and evolution.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Yes, there are mesas in the Jamison Valley in the Blue Mountains of New South Wales, Australia. A mesa is a flat-topped, elevated landform with steep cliffs or sides. In the Jamison Valley, mesas are formed from the erosion of the sandstone plateau that surrounds the valley. Over time, the softer rock layers have been eroded away, leaving behind the harder, more resistant sandstone formations that rise above the surrounding landscape. These mesas offer stunning views of the valley and provide a unique geological and geographical feature in the area.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From Sublime Point Lookout in the Blue Mountains of New South Wales, Australia, visitors can see several notable landmarks in the Jamison Valley below. Some of the most famous landmarks that can be seen from the lookout include:\
